So $ P(z) = z^3 + z + 1 = z^3 + z^1 + 1^0 $, yes.\n\nSo $ \\prod_{\\zeta^7=1} ( \\zeta^3 + \\zeta + 1 ) $, same as before.\n\nNow, the resultant of $ f(z) = z^3 + z + 1 $ and $ g(z) = z^7 - 1 $ is indeed $ \\prod_{f(\\alpha)=0} g(\\alpha) = \\prod_{\\alpha^3+\\alpha+1=0} (\\alpha^7 - 1) $, and we computed that as $-24$.\n\nBut let's verify with a smaller case to see if the method works.\n\nSuppose instead 7 were 3, and $ \\omega = e^{2\\pi i/3} $, compute $ \\prod_{k=0}^2 ( \\omega^{3k} + \\omega^k + 1 ) $. But $ \\omega^3 = 1 $, $ \\omega^{3k} = ( \\omega^3 )^k = 1 $, so each term is $ 1 + \\omega^k + 1 = 2 + \\omega^k $. So product is $ \\prod_{k=0}^2 (2 + \\omega^k) = (2+1)(2+\\omega)(2+\\omega^2) $. Since $ \\omega^2 + \\omega + 1 = 0 $, and $ (z - (-2)) $ evaluated at roots of $ z^2 + z + 1 = 0 $, but better: let $ h(z) = z^3 - 1 = (z-1)(z^2 + z + 1) $, and the product $ \\prod (2 + \\zeta) $ for $\\zeta^3=1$ is $ \\prod ( \\zeta - (-2) ) = (-1)^3 h(-2) $? No.\n\nIf $ h(z) = \\prod (z - \\zeta_i) = z^3 - 1 $, then $ \\prod (a - \\zeta_i) = h(a) = a^3 - 1 $. So $ \\prod (a + \\zeta_i) = \\prod ( \\zeta_i - (-a) ) = (-1)^3 h(-a) = - [ (-a)^3 - 1 ] = - [ -a^3 - 1 ] = a^3 + 1 $\n\nSo $ \\prod_{k=0}^2 (a + \\omega^k) = a^3 + 1 $\n\nFor $a=2$, $ \\prod (2 + \\omega^k) = 8 + 1 = 9 $\n\nCompute manually: $k=0$: $2+1=3$, $k=1$: $2+\\omega$, $k=2$: $2+\\omega^2$, product: $3 (2+\\omega)(2+\\omega^2) = 3 [4 + 2(\\omega+\\omega^2) + \\omega^3 ] = 3[4 + 2(-1) + 1] = 3[4-2+1] = 3*3=9$, correct.\n\nNow using resultant method: $ f(z) = z^3 + z + 1 $, but in this case we have $ \\prod (2 + \\zeta) $, but for our purpose, suppose we had $ \\prod_{\\zeta^3=1} ( \\zeta^3 + \\zeta + 1 ) $. But $ \\zeta^3 = 1 $, so $ \\zeta^3 + \\zeta + 1 = 1 + \\zeta + 1 = 2 + \\zeta $, same as above. But we want to use the resultant of $ f(z) = z^3 + z + 1 $ and $ g(z) = z^3 - 1 $. Then $ \\prod_{\\zeta: g(\\zeta)=0} f(\\zeta) = \\prod_{\\alpha: f(\\alpha)=0} g(\\alpha) $\n\nLeft side: $ \\prod_{\\zeta^3=1} ( \\zeta^3 + \\zeta + 1 ) = \\prod (1 + \\zeta + 1) = \\prod (2 + \\zeta) = 9 $, as above.\n\nRight side: $ \\prod_{\\alpha^3 + \\alpha + 1 = 0} ( \\alpha^3 - 1 ) $\n\nLet $ r $ be root of $ r^3 = -r - 1 $, then $ r^3 - 1 = -r - 1 - 1 = -r -2 $\n\nSo $ \\prod (r^3 - 1) = \\prod (-r -2) = (-1)^3 \\prod (r + 2) = - \\prod (r + 2) = - [ (-1)^3 f(-2) ] = - [ - ( (-8) + (-2) + 1 ) ] = - [ - (-9) ] = - [9] = -9 $\n\nBut we should have 9, but we got -9! Contradiction.\n\nWhat's wrong?\n\nAh! The resultant $\\text{Res}(f,g)$ for $f(z) = z^3 + z + 1$, $g(z) = z^3 - 1$, both monic, is $\\prod_{f(\\alpha)=0} g(\\alpha) = \\prod ( \\alpha^3 - 1 )$, which we computed as $-9$, but the other way is $\\prod_{g(\\zeta)=0} f(\\zeta) = 9$, so $ -9 = 9 $? No.\n\nBut they should be equal. Unless I miscalculated.\n\n$ \\prod_{g(\\zeta)=0} f(\\zeta) = f(1) f(\\omega) f(\\omega^2) $\n\n$f(1) = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3$\n\n$f(\\omega) = \\omega^3 + \\omega + 1 = 1 + \\omega + 1 = 2 + \\omega$\n\n$f(\\omega^2) = (\\omega^2)^3 + \\omega^2 + 1 = 1 + \\omega^2 + 1 = 2 + \\omega^2$\n\nSo product: $3 (2+\\omega)(2+\\omega^2) = 3(4 + 2(\\omega+\\omega^2) + \\omega^3) = 3(4 + 2(-1) + 1) = 3(3) = 9$\n\nNow $ \\prod_{f(\\alpha)=0} g(\\alpha) = \\prod ( \\alpha^3 - 1 ) = \\prod ( (- \\alpha - 1) - 1 ) = \\prod ( - \\alpha -2 ) $ since $ \\alpha^3 = - \\alpha - 1 $\n\nSo $ \\alpha^3 - 1 = - \\alpha - 1 - 1 = - \\alpha -2 $\n\nSo $ \\prod ( \\alpha^3 - 1 ) = \\prod ( - \\alpha -2 ) = (-1)^3 \\prod ( \\alpha + 2 ) = - \\prod ( \\alpha + 2 ) $\n\nNow $ \\prod ( \\alpha + 2 ) = \\prod ( \\alpha - (-2) ) = (-1)^3 f(-2) = - [ (-8) + (-2) + 1 ] = - [-9] = 9 $\n\nSo $ - \\times 9 = -9 $\n\nSo resultant should be both 9 and -9, impossible.\n\nThe issue is that the resultant is defined as the product of the differences only when you account for leading coefficients properly, but here both are monic.\n\nI recall that for two polynomials, the resultant is \\prod_{f} g(\\alpha) = a^{\\deg g} \\prod_{f(\\alpha)=0} g(\\alpha) where a is leading coefficient of f, but since both monic, a=1.\n\nBut in this case, we have a contradiction.\n\nUnless the resultant is not always equal to \\prod_{ roots of f} g(root) for this purpose.\n\nNo, the resultant of two polynomials f and g is defined as the product of the differences of the roots, but with a sign based on degrees.\n\nSpecifically, if f and g are monic, then \\text{Res}(f,g) = \\prod_{f(\\alpha)=0} g(\\alpha) = (-1)^{\\deg f \\deg g} \\prod_{g(\\beta)=0} f(\\beta)\n\nAh! That's it! I forgot the sign.\n\nSo in general, \\text{Res}(f,g) = a^{\\deg g} \\prod f(\\text{roots of }g) = b^{\\deg f} \\prod g(\\text{roots of } f) , and for monic f,g, a=b=1, but there is a sign: \\prod_{f} g(\\alpha) = (-1)^{mn} \\prod_{g} f(\\beta) where m=deg f, n=deg g.\n\nIn our case, f(z) = z^3 + z + 1, deg m=3, g(z) = z^7 - 1, deg n=7, so \\prod_{f(\\alpha)=0} g(\\alpha) = (-1)^{3 \\times 7} \\prod_{g(\\beta)=0} f(\\beta) = (-1)^{21} \\prod = -1 \\times \\prod_{k} f(\\omega^k)\n\nBut also, \\prod_{f(\\alpha)=0} g(\\alpha) = \\prod_{\\alpha} ( \\alpha^7 - 1 )\n\nSo:\n$$\n\\prod_{k=0}^6 f(\\omega^k) = (-1)^{21} \\prod_{\\alpha} ( \\alpha^7 - 1 ) = - \\prod_{\\alpha} ( \\alpha^7 - 1 )\n$$\n\nSince 21 is odd.\n\nIn our earlier calculation, we had \\prod_{\\alpha} ( \\alpha^7 - 1 ) = -24, so then:\n\n\\prod_{k} f(\\omega^k) = - ( -24 ) = 24\n\nYes! So the product is 24.\n\nIn the small case with n=3, f(z)=z^3+z+1, g(z)=z^3-1, then m=3, n=3, (-1)^{9} = -1, and \\prod_{g} f = 9, \\prod_{f} g = -9, and -9 = -1 * 9, yes.\n\nSo in our case, \\prod_{k} f(\\omega^k) = (-1)^{3*7} \\prod_{\\alpha} ( \\alpha^7 - 1 ) = (-1)^{21} (-24) = (-1)(-24) = 24\n\nTherefore, the product is 24.\n\nSo the answer is \\boxed{24}",
