      "generated_answer": "We are given that $ n + 2 $ divides $ 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) $, and we want the sum of all positive integers $ n $ satisfying this condition.\n\nLet $ d = n + 2 $, so $ n = d - 2 $. Substituting, the expression becomes:\n$$\n3((d - 2) + 3)((d - 2)^2 + 9) = 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 4 + 9) = 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13)\n$$\nSo $ d \\mid 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $. Since $ d $ divides this expression, and $ d \\mid d \\cdot (\\text{something}) $, we note that $ d $ must divide the remainder when the expression is reduced modulo $ d $. But modulo $ d $, we have:\n$$\n3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) \\equiv 3(1)(13) = 39 \\pmod{d}\n$$\nThus, $ d \\mid 39 $. The positive divisors of 39 are $ 1, 3, 13, 39 $. Since $ n = d - 2 > 0 $, we require $ d \\geq 3 $. So valid $ d $ values are $ 3, 13, 39 $, giving $ n = 1, 11, 37 $. All are positive integers.\n\nCheck each:\n- $ n = 1 $: $ n+2 = 3 $, product = $ 3(4)(10) = 120 $, $ 3 \\mid 120 $: yes.\n- $ n = 11 $: $ n+2 = 13 $, product = $ 3(14)(130) = 5460 $, $ 13 \\mid 5460 $? $ 5460 / 13 = 420 $: yes.\n- $ n = 37 $: $ n+2 = 39 $, product = $ 3(40)(1378) = 165360 $, $ 165360 / 39 = 4240 $: yes.\n\nSum: $ 1 + 11 + 37 = 49 $.\n\n\\boxed{49}",
