      "generated_answer": "The function $f(i,j)$ is defined recursively. We compute the first few rows of values to identify a pattern:\n- Row 0: $f(0, j) = \\text{mod}_5(j+1)$, so the values are $(1, 2, 3, 4, 0)$.\n- Row 1: Using the recursion, the values are $(2, 3, 4, 0, 1)$.\n- Row 2: The values are $(3, 0, 2, 4, 1)$.\n- Row 3: The values are $(0, 3, 4, 1, 0)$.\n- Row 4: The values are $(3, 1, 3, 1, 3)$.\n- Row 5: $f(5, 0) = f(4, 1) = 1$. Then $f(5, j) = f(4, f(5, j-1))$. Since $f(5, 0)=1$, $f(5, 1) = f(4, 1) = 1$, $f(5, 2) = f(4, 1) = 1$, etc. Thus, Row 5 is $(1, 1, 1, 1, 1)$.\nSince Row 5 consists entirely of 1s, for any $i \\ge 5$, $f(i, j) = f(i-1, f(i, j-1)) = f(i-1, 1) = 1$. Thus, $f(i, j) = 1$ for all $i \\ge 5$ and $0 \\le j \\le 4$. Since $2015 \\ge 5$, we have $f(2015, 2) = 1$.\n\n\\boxed{1}",
