    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "When expanding $(m+n)^6$ using the binomial theorem, the terms are of the form $\\binom{6}{k} m^{6-k} n^k$. Since $m$ and $n$ are odd, their powers remain odd, and the parity of each term depends only on $\\binom{6}{k}$: the term is odd if and only if $\\binom{6}{k}$ is odd.\n\nThe binomial coefficients for $k = 0$ to $6$ are: $1, 6, 15, 20, 15, 6, 1$. Among these, the odd ones are $1, 15, 15, 1$, which occur at $k = 0, 2, 4, 6$. So, there are 4 odd terms.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
