      "generated_answer": "The energy stored in an inductor is given by $ W = \\int_0^i v(t)i(t) \\, dt $, but since $ v(t) = \\frac{d\\phi}{dt} $ and $ \\phi(i) = \\frac{1}{3}i^3 $, we use $ v = \\frac{d\\phi}{di} \\cdot \\frac{di}{dt} = i^2 \\cdot \\frac{di}{dt} $. Given $ i(t) = 2\\sin t $, we compute the energy as $ W = \\int_0^1 v(t)i(t) \\, dt = \\int_0^1 \\left(\\frac{d\\phi}{di} \\cdot \\frac{di}{dt}\\right)i \\, dt $. Instead, recall that energy can be computed as $ W = \\int_0^{i(1)} \\phi(i) \\, di = \\int_0^{2\\sin(1)} \\frac{1}{3}i^3 \\, di = \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{(2\\sin(1))^4}{4} $. But simpler: energy stored is $ W = \\int_0^{i(t)} \\phi(i) \\, di $, but correctly, since $ \\phi = \\frac{1}{3}i^3 $, then $ \\frac{dW}{di} = v i \\cdot dt/di $? Wait — better approach: the energy in a nonlinear inductor is $ W = \\int_0^{i(t)} \\phi(i) \\, di $? No — actually, $ W = \\int_0^t v(t)i(t) \\, dt $, and $ v = \\frac{d\\phi}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt} \\left( \\frac{1}{3}i^3 \\right) = i^2 \\frac{di}{dt} $. So $ W = \\int_0^1 i^2 \\frac{di}{dt} \\cdot i \\, dt = \\int_0^1 i^3 \\frac{di}{dt} dt $. But $ i(t) = 2\\sin t $, so $ \\frac{di}{dt} = 2\\cos t $, $ i^3 = 8 \\sin^3 t $, so $ W = \\int_0^1 8\\sin^3 t \\cdot 2\\cos t \\, dt = 16 \\int_0^1 \\sin^3 t \\cos t \\, dt $. Let $ u = \\sin t $, $ du = \\cos t \\, dt $. When $ t=0 $, $ u=0 $; $ t=1 $, $ u=\\sin 1 $. So $ W = 16 \\int_0^{\\sin 1} u^3 \\, du = 16 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin^4 1}{4} = 4 \\sin^4(1) $. $ \\sin(1 \\text{ rad}) \\approx \\sin(57.3^\\circ) \\approx 0.8415 $, so $ \\sin^4(1) \\approx (0.8415)^4 \\approx 0.5 $, so $ 4 \\cdot 0.5 = 2.00 $. Thus, energy is approximately 2.00 J.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
