      "generated_answer": "The child presents with signs of croup (laryngotracheobronchitis), including a barking cough, stridor, and respiratory distress, which is common in toddlers and often preceded by a viral upper respiratory infection. Despite supportive care, persistent stridor at rest and hypoxia indicate moderate to severe croup, warranting corticosteroid treatment to reduce airway inflammation. The most appropriate next step is oral dexamethasone therapy, which is effective in reducing symptoms and the need for further intervention.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
