      "generated_answer": "The transport of carbon monoxide (CO) is diffusion limited primarily because it binds very avidly to hemoglobin (Hb), which maintains a very low partial pressure gradient of free CO in the blood, thus favoring continuous diffusion across the alveolar membrane. This high affinity for Hb means that most CO is rapidly bound upon entering the blood, minimizing its dissolved form and sustaining the diffusion gradient. Hence, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{A}",
