      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{B}\n\nIn uncontrolled diabetes mellitus, the lack of insulin removes the inhibitory effect on hormone-sensitive lipase, increasing the breakdown of fat into free fatty acids which the liver converts to VLDL. Concurrently, insulin deficiency leads to decreased activity of lipoprotein lipase, the enzyme responsible for clearing triglycerides from the blood. This combination of increased VLDL production and decreased clearance results in hypertriglyceridemia.",
