    "global index": "MusicTheoryBench_254",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following note sequences has the duration equal to an eighth note?\n\nOptions: \nA. L:1/16\nM:none\nK:C\n  (6:4:6B/B/B/B/B/B/ z2 z4 z8 |] %1 \nB. L:1/16\nM:none\nK:C\n  (7:4:7AAAAAAA z4 z8 |] %1\nC. L:1/16\nM:none\nK:C\n  AAAA z4 z8 |] %1\nD. L:1/16\nM:none\nK:C\n  (3A/A/A/ z z2 z4 z8 |] %1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is C. Each note in the sequence \"AAAA\" is a 16th note (due to L:1/16), so four 16th notes equal 4 × 1/16 = 1/4 of a whole note, which is equivalent to two eighth notes. However, the question asks for a sequence with duration equal to *one* eighth note, which is 1/8. Only option C has four 16th notes (AAAA) followed by rests, and since 4 × 1/16 = 1/4, that’s too long — wait, reconsider.\n\nActually, the grouping matters. Option D has \"(3A/A/A/\", which represents three 16th notes under a triplet. In a triplet, three notes take the space of two, so each is (2/3)×(1/16) = 1/24, and three make 3×(1/24)=1/8 — exactly one eighth note. Thus, D is correct.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
