    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4502",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\nLiteracy rates among the Russian-speaking population of late Imperial Russia and the Soviet Union, 1897–1955\nLiteracy Rate\n1897  24%\n1917  45%\n1926  56%\n1937  75%\n1939  81.10%\n1955  99.90%\nSource: Census data and Soviet Ministry of Education\nWhich of the following segments of the Russian/Soviet population would probably have benefited most from the rising literacy rates depicted in the chart?\n\nOptions: \nA. Industrial laborers in major cities\nB. Officers in the armed forces\nC. The urban middle class\nD. Members of the Russian Orthodox clergy\nE. The Russian nobility\nF. Foreign diplomats stationed in Russia\nG. Russian-speaking immigrants in non-Russian territories\nH. The rural peasantry\nI. Members of the Russian Imperial family\nJ. Artists and writers of the time\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The rising literacy rates in late Imperial Russia and the Soviet Union would have most significantly impacted groups previously underserved by education systems, such as the rural peasantry, who were historically marginalized and had limited access to schooling. Soviet policies under Lenin and especially Stalin emphasized mass literacy campaigns, which targeted the largely illiterate peasant population. Thus, the group that benefited most was the rural peasantry.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
