      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{C}\n\nExplanation: The passage acknowledges that the British previously tolerated the practice of sati due to political expediency and concerns for the \"safety of the British Empire,\" rather than an immediate desire to impose British cultural norms. This historical hesitation contradicts the assumption that European colonizers were \"uniformly eager\" to force their culture on native populations, suggesting instead that such interventions were often calculated risks based on the stability of imperial rule.",
